{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "head"}}{{> "head-feed"}}{{/content}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 11 &middot; a blog</p>
    <h1>{{config.site.name}}</h1>
    <p>
      {{model.tally.posts}}, three to a page, filed under {{model.tally.tags}}, with an RSS
      feed and a <code>_redirects</code> file that keeps every address the journal had before
      it moved to <code>/journal/</code>, and the one post that was renamed. Four recipes a
      blog needs and nothing else.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="stack">
    {{#each model.latest}}{{> "post-card"}}{{else}}<p class="meta">Nothing in the journal yet. The first post will be here.</p>{{/each}}
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <a href="{{link 'journal'}}">Read the whole journal</a> &middot;
      <a href="{{link 'journal/tags'}}">Browse by tag</a> &middot;
      <a href="/feed.xml">Subscribe by RSS</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <strong>Posts are a folder fan-out.</strong> One JSON file per post in
    <code>models/posts/</code>, one page each from <code>journal/post.hbs</code>, and
    <code>controllers/post.js</code> between them: it validates the record, derives the slug,
    and sets <code>title</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>date</code> on the
    <em>page</em> so the feed, <code>llms.txt</code> and the layout all read the same values.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The listing is paginated.</strong> Three posts to a page: <code>/journal/</code> and
    <code>/journal/page/2/</code>, prev and next links, one <code>.page()</code> call per page of
    posts from a loop in the build script. The arithmetic is eight lines and it is in the
    script rather than in a controller, because the number of pages is a property of the
    whole collection and a controller only ever sees one record.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tag pages are a second fan-out.</strong> A tag is not a record anybody writes; it
    exists because some posts mention it. <code>controllers/tag.js</code> folds the posts into
    one record per tag and <code>.pages()</code> builds a page from each, exactly as the posts
    themselves were built.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Every internal link is asked for by identity.</strong> No view in this site writes a
    URL. <code>\{{link 'journal'}}</code> renders <code>{{link 'journal'}}</code>,
    <code>\{{link 'journal' absolute=true}}</code> renders
    <code>{{link 'journal' absolute=true}}</code> for an OG tag or a feed, and
    <code>\{{link 'journal' canonical=true}}</code> renders <code>{{link 'journal' canonical=true}}</code>,
    the pretty form the sitemap emits. The ids are in <code>AIKB/site-map.md</code>; an id no page
    claims fails the build rather than shipping a 404.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The feed comes from the registry.</strong> <code>.feed()</code> reads the same page
    list <code>.sitemap()</code> and <code>.llms()</code> read, so an item can never name a URL
    this site does not serve. <code>section: 'journal'</code> keeps the home page out of it; the
    undated listing and tag pages fall out on their own.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
